      "question": "Find the value of $x$ if: $\\dfrac{5^{x-1} \\cdot 3^{2x-3}}{15^{x-2}} = 1$.",
      "answer": "No integer solution for $x$ ($3^{x-1} = \\frac{1}{5}$). If denominator were $15^{x-1}$, then $x = 2$.",
      "options": [],
      "glassboxSteps": [
        {
          "rule": "Factorise 15",
          "why": "$15 = 5 \\times 3$.",
          "math": "15^{x-2} = 5^{x-2} \\cdot 3^{x-2}"
        },
        {
          "rule": "Quotient rule",
          "why": "Subtract exponents for each base.",
          "math": "5^{1} \\cdot 3^{x-1} = 1"
        },
        {
          "rule": "Analyse",
          "why": "$5 \\cdot 3^{x-1} = 1$ implies $3^{x-1} = \\frac{1}{5}$, which has no integer solution.",
          "math": "3^{x-1} = \\dfrac{1}{5}"
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "$5 \\cdot 3^{x-1} = 1$ → no integer $x$. Note: if denominator were $15^{x-1}$, then $x=2$.",
      "linked_note_id": "CH02-sec-exponents-and-powers",
      "source": "practice-session"
